                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Since this is one of the first ways to pass on knowledge, at *History Tracers* we will also do exercises with the body. This helps us reinforce our mathematical knowledge.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_game",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Clapping\n\nWhen we clap, we make a sound. This sound depends on how fast we bring our hands together. We can also clap many times, creating rhythms that help us make music.\n\nClapping is linked to different cultures. It is even used in games that help people's intellectual development. One culture that uses this resource in teaching is the Shona culture in Zimbabwe (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>).\n\nIn this text, we will take the first steps in using clapping to learn. First, choose how many times the hands will clap. Then, choose the speed of the claps and a color for the hands. **Note that, whatever the color, the claps will be the same, because we are all equal, whatever our color** (<htcite3>; <htcite4>). We are all equal because we appeared in Africa about 300,000 years ago (<htcite5>). Skin colors are different mainly because of the amount of melanin people have and the place where they live. Darker skin is more common near the equator — the imaginary line that divides the Earth in half — and lighter skin is more common in farther regions (<htcite6>).\n\nFinally, press the play button (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-play\" onclick=\"startClap()\" style=\"font-size:1em;color:#0f0;\" ></i>) and repeat the claps.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "31070f9b-d4f7-46ed-8ca3-88dd8dee793c",
                     "text": "Nyota, S., & Mapara, J.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2008",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
